DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     TThhiiss iinntteerrffaaccee iiss oobbssoolleetteedd bbyy setpriority(22)..

     The nniiccee() function adds _i_n_c_r to the scheduling priority of the process.
     The priority is a value in the range -20 to 20.  The default priority is
     0; lower priorities cause more favorable scheduling.  Only the super-user
     may lower priorities.

     Children inherit the priority of their parent processes via fork(2).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     Upon successful completion, nniiccee() returns 0, and _e_r_r_n_o is unchanged.
     Otherwise, -1 is returned, the process' nice value is not changed, and
     _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The nniiccee() function will fail if:

     [EPERM]            The _i_n_c_r argument is negative and the caller does not
                        have appropriate privileges.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     nice(1), fork(2), setpriority(2), renice(8)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The nniiccee() function conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”) except
     for the return value.  This implementation returns 0 upon successful com‐
     pletion but the standard requires returning the new nice value, which
     could be -1.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A nniiccee() syscall appeared in Version 6 AT&T UNIX.

